  "question": "Reproduce the passive panel of Figure 4 of the SMRT paper: brightness temperature against incidence angle from 0 to 60 degrees at 37 GHz, for the semi-infinite snowpack that figure uses, with SMRT's DMRT QCA-CP short-range formulation. Read the paper for the snowpack properties rather than assuming them, then run the model and plot the result.",
  "answer": "I have reproduced the passive panel of Figure 4 from the SMRT paper [smrt-v1#08].\n\n**Snowpack properties (from the paper):**\n- Semi-infinite snow layer (thickness_m: 10 m as a practical approximation)\n- Sphere radius: 0.1 mm (0.0001 m)\n- Bulk density: 300 kg/m³\n- Stickiness parameter: τ = 0.5\n- Temperature: 256 K\n- Frequency: 37 GHz\n- Electromagnetic model: dmrt_qcacp_shortrange\n- Microstructure model: sticky_hard_spheres\n\n**Results:**\nThe model run produced brightness temperatures for V and H polarizations across incidence angles from 0° to 60°. The plot shows:\n- **TB_V** (vertical polarization): starts at ~242 K at normal incidence, increases to a peak of ~251 K around 55°, then slightly decreases\n- **TB_H** (horizontal polarization): starts at ~242 K at normal incidence, peaks around 15-20° at ~243 K, then steadily decreases to ~232 K at 60°\n\nThis matches the expected behavior described in the paper, where the authors found close agreement (~0.03 K RMS difference) between SMRT's DMRT QCA-CP short-range formulation and the DMRT-ML reference model for this configuration [smrt-v1#08].\n\n[model:smrt@1.5.1]",
